euler-lagrange-generalized-coordinates.conceptual_definition:
The Euler-Lagrange equation for generalized coordinates, 
 \tau_i = \frac{d}{dt} \left( \frac{\partial L}{\partial \dot{\theta}_i} \right) - \frac{\partial L}{\partial \theta_i} 
 is a fundamental equation of motion in classical mechanics derived from the principle of least action. It provides a generalized way to express the dynamics of a system using generalized coordinates, which can be any set of independent variables that uniquely define the configuration of the system. This equation is particularly powerful because it can be applied to systems with constraints and can simplify the analysis of complex mechanical problems by reducing the number of equations of motion needed.
